A||||2||batboy||CV joints
Z||000000||batboy||07-18-99||12:51 AM||||While working on my rear brakes, I noticed one of the CV boots was ripped. I know that means bad news, because moisture and dirt gets in. I twisted the axles back and forth, and there seem like a lot of play to me, but maybe there's suppose to be some play in the transmission. On a front wheel drive car, worn CV joints will click and make noise when you turn sharply. How do you tell if the CV joints in a Fiero are going bad?
Z||000001||lowCG||07-18-99||02:35 AM||||Usually a rumbling,or vibration,noticeable as you get on,or off the throttle.Might be hard to tell with the truck-style motormounts you're probably using on that V8 car.
Z||000002||theogre||07-19-99||12:20 PM||||the joint has enough miles on it you should probably just replace the shaft. By the time you fool with regreasing, and replacing the boot, you aren't saving time, and you still have a joint that may not be all that good. Rebuilt shafts are in stock at many places, and cost around 90-100$us. There is a hefty core charge on them, so take the old one with you. Make sure you save the guard ring off the outer joint, the new shaft may not come with one. Be carefull not to beat the hell out of the ring.
